{"kind":"propose","text":"What a behaviour-describing statement rests on, and what makes a basis die.\n\nTwo rules, one on each side of the gap recorded in the incident this rests on.\n\nRule one, for the author. A statement that describes how the system behaves rests on the acts that govern that behaviour — the artifact statements for the implementation it describes — and not only on the task that produced the document. The test for a basis is whether retiring it would mean the prose needs re-checking. A task request and its review report both fail that test: they stay true forever, whatever the code does next, which is exactly why the documentation merged in July still projects as current while describing behaviour the repository no longer has.\n\nRule two, for whoever changes the world. An artifact statement that replaces an earlier artifact for the same path supersedes it, explicitly, as part of the same change. This is what creates the death that rule one depends on. Without it artifacts accumulate, none dies, and everything resting on them projects current forever, which is the state we are in now. The burden lands at the point of change rather than on the memory of the document author, and the act that carries it — the artifact statement — is one discipline 8 already requires.\n\nExplicit, not inferred. The fold must not treat a later artifact for the same path as implicitly retiring the earlier one. Path is a free string in the body, and the log already holds artifacts recorded at a bare dot, at comma-joined lists, and at absolute worktree paths, so implicit matching would be guesswork; it would also retroactively change how existing history projects. More decisively, it would be the substrate inventing meaning, which is the one thing it does not do.\n\nThe projector makes the omission visible instead. Three marks:\n\nDescribes a superseded world. When the retired ancestor that made a statement stale is itself an artifact, the staleness concerns the world the statement describes, not the argument it stands on. Both are staleness; only one means go and re-read the code. The distinction is computable from what is already recorded, and it is the distinction the request asks for.\n\nUnable to flare. A statement with no basis can never go stale, so the projection must not let its silence read as currency. Artifacts with an empty rests_on carry this mark.\n\nSuccession not recorded. A new artifact for a path whose predecessor is still live is a probable forgotten supersession. This is a legibility warning about practice, not a verdict on the act — the act stays effective — and it is what keeps rule two from decaying into another thing everyone means to do.\n\nWhat this costs, stated plainly rather than discovered later. A page resting on an artifact will flare whenever that artifact is superseded, including for changes that do not touch what the page says. A flare therefore means re-check this, not this is wrong, and the documentation must say so where readers meet the marks. That is a real and recurring maintenance load, and it is the trade being proposed: visible work in place of silent falsity. Resting prose on ratified decisions instead would flare less often, but behaviour frequently lands without a ratified decision, whereas discipline 8 already requires an artifact for every implementing change. Artifacts are the basis that reliably exists.\n\nThree limits stay open under this proposal and belong wherever it is documented, rather than being left for a reader to find. Staleness does not propagate through ineffective bases, so a chain passing through an ineffective act goes quiet instead of stale; that is carried by the lying-by-omission request. Nothing checks that a cited commit carries the Rests-On trailer that would bridge it. And a free-standing rests_on is not validated for resolvability — only the request, promise and report chain is enforced, as the correction this rests on records — so a statement can cite a basis that does not exist and project as current. The unable-to-flare mark is what stands between those limits and false confidence, which is why it belongs in this change and not a later one.\n\nScope if adopted: SKILL.md discipline 8 and the AGENTS.md repository loop gain the two rules; the projector gains the three marks with tests, including a multi-hop case and a case where the retired ancestor is not an artifact; the kernel is untouched and no admission rule changes."}